    "answer": {
      "short": "Mary is correct. The inner expression $\\frac{1}{x}$ must be computed first, requiring $x\\ne0$. The domain remains $(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.",
      "worked": "Mary is correct. Computing the inner expression $\\frac{1}{x}$ requires $x\\ne0$. For every such input, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is also nonzero, so the outer reciprocal is defined.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{1}{1/x},\\qquad x\\ne0\\\\f(x)&=x,\\qquad x\\ne0\\end{align*}$$\n\nSimplifying the expression does not restore the excluded input $0$. Therefore, the domain is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
